# Line-ending policy for this repository.
#
# Nothing here is consumed by a Windows-only tool: the payload is Markdown
# skills, JSON manifests, Python and shell scripts, read by git, GitHub, Claude
# Code and a Linux server. So the working tree is pinned LF on every platform,
# and per-user core.autocrlf / core.eol settings cannot change that.
#
# Without this file the LF-ness of the tree is luck. Windows git defaults to
# core.autocrlf=true, so one contributor editing a shipped .sh is enough to
# break it — see the *.sh note below.

# Classify text vs binary by content at add time; store LF in the repository so
# diffs and blame stay clean; check out LF on disk and convert back on add.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts are LF, always and without exception.
# skills/ss13-tgs-deploy/assets/*.sh are copied onto a Linux host and executed
# there. `#!/usr/bin/env bash` followed by CRLF makes the kernel search for an
# interpreter literally named "bash\r"; the script dies with a "not found" that
# names a binary that plainly exists, which is a genuinely confusing failure to
# debug from a deploy log. This line is the whole defence.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Git hooks are shell scripts without a .sh extension, so the rule above does not
# name them. Same failure as the shipped deploy scripts: a CRLF after the shebang
# makes the kernel look for an interpreter called "sh\r". The hook then silently
# does nothing on every commit, which is a defect that reports itself as silence.
scripts/hooks/* text eol=lf

# Binaries: never line-end converted, never diffed as text. `text=auto` usually
# detects these, but detection is content-based and an odd header can fool it.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.dmi binary
*.zip binary
*.skill binary
*.docx binary
*.pdf binary

# Adding or changing this file only affects content as it passes through git.
# An existing checkout must be rewritten once:
#
#   git add .gitattributes
#   git add --renormalize .
#   git status        # review, then commit
#
# --renormalize rewrites the INDEX, not files already on disk. To fix a
# straggler in the working tree, delete and re-checkout it, then count:
#   tr -dc '\r' < <path> | wc -c      # must be 0
